FINDINGS:
1. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:1856 — A non-list argument is reported as `"zip requires at least two lists"` even when three or more lists were supplied (`zip([1], 2, [3])`), so the message names the wrong fault; the new test at tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs:2322-2348 only asserts `.contains("zip")`, so it passes on this incorrect message. — A caller is told to fix arity when the real problem is an item type, and the test cannot detect the misleading text. — Return `"zip requires lists"` (type fault) from the loop and keep `"zip requires at least two lists"` only for the under-arity check; tighten the test to assert the two distinct messages.

2. [MINOR] src/stdlib.rs:1382 — `BUILTIN_SIGNATURES` describes `chain` as `chain(list, list, ...)`, which implies at least two lists, but `chain()` (empty) and `chain([1])` (one list) are valid and tested (src/runtime.rs:1837-1847; tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs:2236-2237). — `:sighelp`/introspection contradicts the behaviour the SPEC and tests pin. — Write the signature as `chain(list, ...)` (zero-or-more) or otherwise state that no lists yields the empty list.

3. [MINOR] tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs:1265-1271 — The new `assert_eq!(walked, compiled)` claims to compare values "as data" to catch engines producing functions whose bodies differ, but `FunctionValue`'s `PartialEq` deliberately ignores the body (src/value.rs:202-205), so for function values this assertion is exactly as blind as the text comparison it was added to strengthen; FINDINGS.md/REPORT.md overstate the coverage. — The resumed-run claim that the cross-engine test now distinguishes differing function bodies is not true. — Either compare a body-sensitive property explicitly or correct the claim/comment to say equality checks only name, params and captures.

